This C++ program implements a doubly linked list with zero-based indexing. It supports inserting an element at a specified position, deleting an element at a specified position, searching for an element by value, and displaying the current contents of the list.

The program manages memory using dynamic allocation with the new operator during insertion and frees memory using the delete operator during removal. A global pointer named head tracks the start of the list.

Time Complexity:

Insertion at position 0: O(1) time complexity.
Insertion at position N: O(N) time complexity because it traverses up to position N - 1.
Deletion at position 0: O(1) time complexity.
Deletion at position N: O(N) time complexity because it traverses up to position N.
Search: O(N) time complexity in the worst case as it scans from head to tail.
Display: O(N) time complexity to traverse and print all elements.
Space Complexity: O(1) auxiliary space per operation, and O(N) overall space to store N nodes.

Sample Output:

    Insert

    Delete

    Search

    Display

    Exit
    Enter your choice: 1
    Enter element and position: 10 0
